   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "By the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, \\( g'(x) = \\sqrt{2}\\cos x - 1 \\) for \\( 0 < x < 2 \\pi \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Add",
            [
               "Multiply",
               [
                  "Sqrt",
                  2
               ],
               [
                  "Cos",
                  "x"
               ]
            ],
            -1
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Set \\( g'(x) = 0 \\): \\( \\sqrt{2}\\cos x - 1 = 0 \\). The solutions in \\( 0 < x < 2 \\pi \\) are \\( x = \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\) and \\( x = \\frac{7 \\pi}{4} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            [
               "Multiply",
               7,
               "Pi"
            ],
            4
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "Only \\( x = \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\) and \\( x = \\frac{7 \\pi}{4} \\) lie in \\( 0 < x < 2 \\pi \\); \\( x = - \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\) is outside the interval on which \\( g \\) is defined, so it is not a critical point of \\( g \\).",
         "mathjson": [
            "Divide",
            [
               "Multiply",
               7,
               "Pi"
            ],
            4
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "\\( g' \\) changes from negative to positive at \\( x = \\frac{7 \\pi}{4} \\) and from positive to negative at \\( x = \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\), so \\( g \\) has its relative minimum at \\( x = \\frac{7 \\pi}{4} \\).",
         "mathjson": [
